{% comment %}
  One row of the event list.

  Extracted so the two sources -- a Seminars collection and hand-authored
  blocks -- render identically. Duplicating the markup in both branches is how
  the fallback quietly stops matching the real thing.

    city        string, required
    date        string, already formatted for display
    focus       string, blank renders the TBA state
    price       string, already money-formatted
    target      href for the fallback link. Ignored when a product is passed
    sold_out    boolean. Renders a flat row with no panel
    cue         buy button text, defaults to "Reserve"
    attrs       block.shopify_attributes, or blank in collection mode
    product     the product object, when there is one
    venue       string, e.g. "Example Gym -- Example City"
    address     string, one line
    map         url
    time        string, free text -- "Sat 9am-5pm . Sun 9am-3pm"

  THE ROW OPENS INSTEAD OF NAVIGATING, and that is the whole point of it.

  It used to be one big <a> to the product page. The only things that page held
  that this list does not are the venue, the address, a map link and the time
  of day -- four facts. Sending someone to a separate page to read four facts,
  and then back, is a page load and a context switch at the exact moment they
  have decided to buy.

  <details>/<summary>, NOT a JavaScript accordion. It opens with no script at
  all, it is keyboard operable and announced as expandable for free, and
  Ctrl+F on a closed panel scrolls to and opens it in current browsers. An
  accordion built by hand gets none of that without work, and gets it wrong.

  WHY THE ROW IS NO LONGER A LINK AT ALL. An <a> cannot contain a <form>, which
  is what forced the old choice between the product page and /cart/{variant}:1
  -- neither of which lands anyone on checkout. A <summary> has no such
  restriction, so the panel holds a real add-to-cart form with return_to, and
  the row finally does what "Reserve" implies. The trade recorded in
  sections/suf-semlist.liquid -- whole-row click target versus a real checkout
  jump -- is settled the other way now, because the row buys something for the
  click it takes: the four facts someone wants before committing to travel.

  PRODUCT IS PASSED AS AN OBJECT, breaking this snippet's usual rule that every
  value arrives resolved. `{% form 'product' %}` needs the object; there is no
  scalar to hand it. Everything else is still resolved by the caller.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign has_details = false
  if venue != blank or address != blank or map != blank or time != blank
    assign has_details = true
  endif

  assign can_buy = false
  if product != blank and sold_out != true
    assign can_buy = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  A row with nothing to reveal and nothing to sell must not render a control
  that opens onto an empty box. It falls back to the flat row, which is also
  what a sold-out event gets.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if sold_out or has_details == false and can_buy == false -%}
  <div class="suf-semrow suf-semrow--flat" {{ attrs }}>
    <div class="suf-semrow__line">
      {%- render 'suf-semcells', city: city, date: date, focus: focus, price: price -%}
      {%- if sold_out -%}
        <span class="suf-semrow__focus suf-semrow__focus--tba">Sold out</span>
      {%- elsif target != blank -%}
        <a class="suf-semrow__cue" href="{{ target }}">
          {{- cue | default: 'Reserve' | escape }}
          <span class="suf-semrow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      {%- else -%}
        <span></span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  <details class="suf-semrow" {{ attrs }}>
    <summary class="suf-semrow__line">
      {%- render 'suf-semcells', city: city, date: date, focus: focus, price: price -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The chevron is the only thing in the row that changes on open, so it is
        what tells someone the control is stateful. aria-hidden because
        <summary> already announces expanded/collapsed itself -- naming it
        again would have a screen reader say the state twice.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        "Details", not the `cue` setting. The cue is the BUY word and it now
        labels the button inside the panel; using it here too would put
        "Reserve" on a control that reserves nothing, which is the same broken
        promise the old row made by linking to a product page.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="suf-semrow__cue">
        Details
        <span class="suf-semrow__chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </span>
    </summary>

    <div class="suf-semrow__panel">
      {%- if has_details -%}
        <dl class="suf-semrow__facts">
          {%- if venue != blank -%}
            <div class="suf-semrow__fact">
              <dt>Where</dt>
              <dd>
                {{ venue | escape }}
                {%- if address != blank -%}
                  <span class="suf-semrow__addr">{{ address | escape }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  The map opens in a new tab deliberately. Someone checking how
                  far the gym is from an airport is mid-purchase, and taking
                  the page away from them is how a considered buy becomes an
                  abandoned one. rel is not optional on target=_blank: without
                  noopener the opened page gets a handle on this one.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- if map != blank -%}
                  <a
                    class="suf-semrow__map"
                    href="{{ map }}"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  >
                    Open in Maps
                    <span class="suf-visually-hidden">(opens in a new tab)</span>
                  </a>
                {%- endif -%}
              </dd>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if time != blank -%}
            <div class="suf-semrow__fact">
              <dt>Hours</dt>
              <dd>{{ time | escape }}</dd>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </dl>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if can_buy -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A REAL add-to-cart, straight to checkout. The date is not a choice
          here -- this row IS the date -- so unlike the bundles there is no
          seminar property to carry: the variant says which seminar it is.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- form 'product', product, class: 'suf-semrow__form' -%}
          <input
            type="hidden"
            name="id"
            value="{{ product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}"
          >
          <input type="hidden" name="return_to" value="/checkout">
          <button type="submit" class="suf-btn suf-btn--primary" name="add">
            {{- cue | default: 'Reserve' | escape }} &middot; {{ price -}}
          </button>
        {%- endform -%}
      {%- elsif target != blank -%}
        <a class="suf-btn suf-btn--primary" href="{{ target }}">
          {{- cue | default: 'Reserve' | escape -}}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </details>
{%- endif -%}
